why 2 0 DOUBLING The leading tone C♯ is doubled in soprano and tenor, but only for an eighth: the tenor moves on to B at once.
why 1 1/2 OVERLAP The bass leaps up a fourth to G while the tenor steps up F♯–G, so they meet in unison. A strong root leap into IV.
why 1 3/4 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Tenor and bass go from a unison G to an octave E, with eighth notes in between: the tenor leaps up B–E while the bass walks G–F♯–E. Bach hears two different lines; in exercises, avoid it.
why 1 3/4 OVERLAP The tenor leaps up to E, above where the alto was, while the alto leaps up to G. Both move up together, so the order stays clear.
why 4 0 DOUBLING As in bar 2, soprano and tenor share the leading tone C♯ on V. The soprano turns around it (C♯–B–C♯–D) and the tenor leaps away to F♯, so the note is never resolved twice.
why 4 1/4 OVERLAP The tenor leaps up to F♯, above where the alto was, while the alto leaps up to A. They move up together.
why 5 1/2 RANGE The new phrase starts with the bass high on D, walking down a scale D–C♯–B–A–G–F♯. Starting high gives the bass room to descend.
why 5 5/8 RANGE The bass is on its way down the scale from D and is back in its usual range on the next beat.
why 6 0 SEVENTH The soprano's A belongs to the D chord, held over the bass's passing B and A. It is not a seventh, and the tune rises to B as written.
why 6 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice falls toward the dominant as the phrase descends: the tenor leaps down E–A to the root of V while the bass steps D–C♯.
why 8 1/2 UNRESOLVED_NCT The alto strikes E again over the D chord and then falls to D: a suspension struck anew instead of tied, resolving down by step.
why 10 1/2 DOUBLING The chord is really F♯ minor with the tenor's B held over as a suspension; it falls to A on the next beat. F♯ is the root, doubled as usual, not a seventh.
